from audit_utils import *

for tree,layer in [('fr_printed','French as printed'),('fr','French corrected')]:
 rel=f'sources/{tree}/fr/212.tex'
 def r(a,b,line,kind='reading',why='',count=1):
  replace(rel,a,b,layer,'212-114',line,'6.2; 6.3; 7.1','400-dpi full scan and all three enlarged bands. '+why,kind,count=count)
 if tree=='fr_printed':
  s,_,_=page_text(rel,114)
  for a,b,body in list(footnotes(s)):
   r(s[a:b],'','Proposition 7.1',kind='structure',why='The scan has no footnote. This is the existing edition correction note and belongs only to the corrected layer.')
  r(r'un $B\otimes_V K$',r'un $B\otimes_V A$','Proposition 7.1, generic fibre',why='The printed second base-change expression unmistakably ends with A. Restore that misprint only in the as-printed layer.')
 else:
  with (ROOT/'CORRECTIONS.tsv').open('a',newline='') as f:
   csv.writer(f,delimiter='\t').writerow([rel,layer,'212-114','Proposition 7.1, generic fibre','7.1',r'B\otimes_V A',r'B\otimes_V K','400-dpi middle band: the print has A. Existing correction confirmed: B is a V-scheme and B prime is its generic fibre over the fraction field K. The factor must be K, as in the preceding A tensor_V K. The existing explicit editorial footnote is retained only in this corrected layer.','misprint of the original','low'])
 r('$R$ provient',r'$\mathcal R$ provient','Theorem 6.2(iii)',why='The whole groupoid letter is script R.')
 r('R_U',r'\mathcal R_U','Theorem 6.2(iii)',count=2,why='The restricted groupoid letter is script R; relation-scheme R in Remark 6.3 stays ordinary.')
 for a,b,line in [('« birationnelle »',r'\fquote{birationnelle}','paragraph following 6.2'),('« non admissible »',r'\fquote{non admissible}','Remark 6.3(2)'),('« modules »',r'\fquote{modules}','section 7 introduction'),('« réduit »',r'\fquote{réduit}','proof of 7.1'),('« adhérence »',r'\fquote{adhérence}','proof of 7.1')]:
  r(a,b,line,why='The phrase has ordinary double quotes.')
 r(r'\textbf{REMARQUES 6.3.}',r'\textnormal{REMARQUES 6.3.}','Remarks heading',why='The standalone heading is roman, not bold.')
 r('effective:', 'effective :','Remark 6.3(2)',why='The colon is spaced.')
 r(r'\fsection{7}{Applications}',r'\fsection{\normalfont 7}{\normalfont\uline{Applications}.}','section 7 heading',why='The title is roman and straight-underlined, with a final period.')
 r('SHIMURA:', 'SHIMURA :','Proposition 7.1 introduction',why='The colon is spaced.')
 r('« se réduit bien pour\n$V$ »,',r'\fquote{se réduit bien pour $V$}','Proposition 7.1',why='The ordinary closing quote is not followed by a comma before i. e.')
 r('i.e.','i. e.','Proposition 7.1 and its proof',count=3,why='All three printed abbreviations are spaced.')
 r('N.B.:','N. B. :','proof of 7.1',why='The letters and colon are spaced.')
 upright_page(rel,layer,'212-114')

event('212-6.2-iii','carried as background without fault found','The effectiveness and faithful flatness assertion on the dense saturated open completes Theorem 6.2. It is retained with the proof obligation recorded on p. 113; reading the assertion is not treated as a proof of it.','212, p. 114, Theorem 6.2(iii)')
event('212-6.3-1','targeted proof check','For an action groupoid, the geometric fibre of (source,target) over (x,y), when nonempty, is a translate of the stabilizer of x. Consequently the finite-fibre condition forces zero-dimensional stabilizers, and finite-type zero-dimensional group schemes over a field are finite. Conversely finite stabilizers make every such geometric fibre finite. This verifies the practical restriction described in the remark. The question about removing it is historical, not asserted to remain open today.','212, p. 114, Remark 6.3(1)')
event('212-6.3-2-affine-example','independent re-proof','Own explicit example supporting the non-effectiveness warning: over a field of characteristic not two let X=A1 and R be the disjoint union of the diagonal A1 and the graph of t maps to -t restricted to Gm, inside X times X. The two branches are disjoint as subfunctors; inversion and composition give an equivalence relation. Its projections are etale and quasi-finite, with fibres of size two off zero and size one at zero, and are not finite. Any morphism h:X to a prescheme that equalizes R is invariant under t maps to -t everywhere: equality holds off zero, and near zero both maps land in a common affine neighbourhood of their common value, where equality follows from schematic density. Thus its coequalizer is the usual finite-group quotient q:t maps to t^2, by the affine finite quotient result 5.1. But X times_q X is Spec k[x,y]/(x^2-y^2); at the origin it has the two branches and is not the diagonal neighbourhood appearing in R. Therefore R is not effective in preschemes. This verifies an independent affine example, not the specific Nagata construction mentioned in the print.','212, p. 114, Remark 6.3(2)')
event('212-6.3-2-Nagata','carried as background without fault found','The reference to affine opens covering Nagata’s variety is retained. The particular variety, involution and gluing argument have not been reconstructed here and require an exact source in the notes. The text’s questions about dropping flatness are recorded as historical questions.','212, p. 114, Remark 6.3(2)')
event('212-7.1-generic-fibre','targeted proof check','The scan prints B tensor_V A but the statement is about B prime over the fraction field K. The generic fibre of a V-scheme B is B tensor_V K, and the preceding A tensor_V K fixes the same convention. Replacing A by K is unambiguous. The existing correction is confirmed independently of its editorial footnote; A is restored only in the as-printed layer.','212, p. 114, Proposition 7.1')
event('212-7.1-closure','independent re-proof','Let C be the schematic closure of C prime in A over the DVR V. Affine-locally its coordinate ring injects into the localization defining C prime, hence is torsion-free over V and therefore flat. Its generic fibre is C prime. Products of these flat V-schemes are flat and have schematically dense generic fibre. Multiplication and inversion preserve C on the generic fibre; the defining ideal therefore pulls back to zero on C times C and C, respectively, by that schematic density. The identity section likewise factors through C. Thus C is a flat closed subgroup scheme; it is proper since A is proper. With projectivity of the abelian scheme over this DVR as a separate background input, the translation equivalence relation C times A on A meets 6.1. This verifies the closure step, not all remaining abelian-scheme assertions on the next page.','212, p. 114, proof of Proposition 7.1')
event('212-7.1-isogeny-reduction','carried as background without fault found','The initial reduction uses that an isogeny of abelian varieties has an isogeny in the reverse direction (a suitable multiple of the inverse in the rational Hom category). Compose a quotient A_K to Q with Q to B prime; its kernel is the possibly nonreduced subgroup C prime. This standard isogeny fact, projectivity over the DVR and essential uniqueness of the abelian model are identified background inputs.','212, p. 114, Proposition 7.1 and first proof sentence')
record_page('212',114,r'''Compared sources/fr_printed/fr/212.tex entry lines 697–749 and sources/fr/fr/212.tex entry lines 700–752 with scan leaf 17 at 400 dpi, full page and all three enlarged bands. Read 6.2(iii), both remarks, the applications introduction, Proposition 7.1 and the proof through the quotient construction.

The existing correction B tensor_V A to B tensor_V K in 7.1 is confirmed by the visible A and by the generic-fibre meaning. The as-printed layer restores A and removes the editorial footnote; the corrected layer retains K and its explicit existing note, with a mathematical correction row. This correction is outside the seventeen entries of the supplied canonical disposition list. Both layers restore the script groupoid letters at the top, roman remarks heading, underlined section heading, ordinary quotation marks, spaced abbreviations and punctuation, and upright mathematical letters. Ordinary R in the non-effectiveness example remains ordinary.

An explicit independent affine equivalence relation proves the phenomenon in Remark 6.3(2); the named Nagata construction remains background. Checked the stabilizer interpretation of 6.3(1) and the flat closed-subgroup property of the schematic closure in 7.1. Isogeny reversal, projectivity and uniqueness of abelian models are clearly identified background inputs; the proof continues on the next page.''')
